Error Types and Meaning

Core 
A Type I error is rejecting a true null (false positive).
A Type II error is failing to reject a false null (false negative).
Power is the probability your test correctly detects the effect when it truly exists.

Type I Error (False Positive)
α = P(Reject H₀ | H₀ is true)

Type II Error (False Negative)
β = P(Fail to reject H₀ | Hₐ is true)
  = P(Fail to reject H₀ | H₀ is false)

Statistical Power
Power = 1 - β
      = P(Reject H₀ | Hₐ is true)

How α, β, and Power Interact
Lowering α (stricter threshold) usually increases β, reducing power.
Increasing sample size reduces β, increasing power.
Larger effect sizes increase power.
Higher variance increases β, reducing power.

